AB  - Fungal pathogenesis is highly non-linear; macroclimates alone cannot predict it because of variations in canopy-level microclimates and cropping cultures. Decades of peer-reviewed empirical data show statistically significant anomalies to climate trends for the Caribbean. Surface air and sea surface temperatures have exhibited a steady warming trend of 0.1°C-0.2°C and 0.2°C-0.5°C per decade, respectively, over the past 50 years. This thermal expansion is accompanied by a shift in regional precipitation, characterized by a higher frequency of consecutive dry days punctuated by severe, episodic convective rainfall events. There has also been a recorded increase in the intensity, behavior, and moisture dynamics of hurricanes and tropical storms in the region. These climate shifts facilitate plant fungal disease infection, severity and spread within and among countries of the Caribbean. In addition, the legacies of land use as well as differences in individual island topology alter the actual disease outcome. Next-generation machine learning pipelines may provide a powerful approach to analysing these complex interactions to inform risk assessment decisions. This manuscript introduces a novel, stratified regional conceptual framework. Economically-important Caribbean fungal and oomycete pathosystems were selected for four proposed classes: Subterranean Matrix, Canopy-Boundary Microclimate, Structural Damage Opportunists, and Biotrophic-Necrotrophic Shift. By mapping fungi and oomycete dispersal and infection mechanics against modern computational capabilities, a conceptual framework for cross-border data integration is proposed. Data hubs for the Caribbean also factor human-in-the-loop governance to preserve transboundary data sovereignty. Ultimately, the rationale for this stratified regional framework supports UN SDG 2 (Zero Hunger/Food Security) and SDG 13 (Climate Action) for tropical agricultural sustainability.
ER  - 

