AB  - Centralised machine learning offers optimised computation while performing data processing, predictions and classification tasks; however, it suffers from challenges related to data privacy, compliance and user trust. The transfer and processing of sensitive data at the centralised trusted system could create a single point of failure, a single point of attack, and introduce significant security and privacy risks, and violate data sovereignty principles. This centralised implementation is not feasible in domains where privacy is a major concern for users and service providers. Federated Learning (FL) offers a decentralised alternative by enabling collaborative model training without sharing raw data with the centralised system or among collaborators. However, FL introduces new vulnerabilities, for example, inference, membership and poisoning attacks. Existing privacy-preserving approaches, such as homomorphic encryption, secure multiparty computation, and differential privacy, either impose computation or communication overheads or fail to guarantee both confidentiality and integrity simultaneously. To address these limitations, we propose a system that incorporates robust mechanisms to facilitate the privacy-preserving aggregation of model updates and ensures that shared updates cannot be exploited to infer sensitive information. To this extent, we leverage secret sharing protocol for the collaborative update of the Machine learning models and incorporate a zero-knowledge proof for the verifiability of the communicated model score and respective aggregated model. The process ensures confidentiality, integrity and verifiability of models communicated with the centralised system under honest but curious and malicious adversaries. It also enables the secure verification of the submitted (or commented) model updates. We present a rigorous security and privacy analysis alongside a comprehensive experimental evaluation using microbenchmarks that quantify computation and communication overhead. The security and privacy analysis shows that the proposed approach achieves strong privacy-preservation and robustness under different threat models. The system overheads are minimal which make it scalable and well-suited for deployment in real-world applications, for example healthcare, finance, and the Internet of Things (IoT).
