AB  - This chapter outlines the core philosophy, methodological architecture, and epistemic foundations of the book. The book presents an integrated ecosystem of seven primary no-code, open-source, and fully local analytic frameworks along with a local multibrain AI interpretive orchestration environment. Together, these analytic frameworks and software tools were designed to help researchers analyze, visualize, integrate, and interpret textual, relational, spatial, temporal, affective, and multimodal evidence. At its core, the book is grounded in the democratizationof data science, wherein open-access data science, machine learning, and artificial intelligence tools can be executed locally (i.e., avoiding the need to upload data to external servers) and their execution are not subjected to computer nor statistical programming literacy—similar to the idea of not having to learn C++ or TypeScript to to start drafting an essay in Microsoft Word. In this chapter we also discuss the construct of a Truly Equal Status Design (TESD), by which we mean a genuinely equal-status mixed methods and multimodal design in which quantitative, computational, and visual outputs do not replace qualitative, mixed, or multimodal evidence but function as maps that guide researchers back into the original materials. Within this ecosystem, our local AI with multiple brains, which we call “Intelligent Systems for Academic Research Integration” (ISARI),provides the interpretive and synthetic layer through which researchers can engage local generative and reasoning AI as a brainstorming partnerfor memoing, comparison, synthesis, and evidence-grounded writing while preserving interpretive authority and data sovereignty. The chapter argues that integrative methods should be understood not as fixed pipelines but as structured, adaptive, and flexible systemsfor generating and validating evidence across modalities. We also provide readers with an overview of the types of data handled across the book and a comparative table clarifying how each software environment contributes to the analysis of qualitative, mixed-methods, and multimodal evidence. Overall, the chapter frames the book as a contribution not only to data science democratization, but also to the design of privacy-conscious, multimodal, ethically-grounded, and interpretively responsible scholarly infrastructures.
