AB  - Cross-border data flow serves as a driving force for digital economic development. However, unregulated cross-border data flows may pose substantial risks including data breaches, privacy violations, and challenges to data sovereignty. Therefore, developing effective strategies to ensure secure and legal cross-border data transmission has become an urgent priority. This paper applies evolutionary game theory to construct a model of interaction between data cross-border transmission institutions and the industrial supervision department, involving the role of third-party regulatory agencies. By analyzing the factors influencing the strategies of both parties through a payoff matrix, this study explores the equilibrium points and their stability. The conclusion highlights that reasonable punitive measures are essential to regulate non-compliance and standardize cross-border data flows. Moreover, third-party regulatory agencies play a critical role in identifying and mitigating non-compliance risks, thereby ensuring adherence to data transmission standards. Numerical simulations are conducted to provide managerial insights and effective strategies to enhance the governance of cross-border data transmission.
