AB  - Public administration relies on structured data distributed across heterogeneous systems. However, access using natural language (NL) to such data remains difficult, especially in lower-resource administrative and organizational settings where data handling must remain local. This paper presents a Romanian natural-language-to-SQL (NL-to-SQL) pipeline for structured-data access in public administration. The proposed method integrates local schema preparation, teacher-guided candidate generation, execution-based SQL validation on local database dumps, bounded repair during data construction, Romanian-language adaptation, semantic consistency checks, staged supervised fine-tuning, and a bounded inference-time SQL repair phase. Translation is used only during data construction to obtain Romanian supervision; after adaptation, the deployed model receives Romanian questions directly and produces SQL without requiring a separate runtime translation component. The resulting workflow supports the adaptation of a compact local language model using domain-specific Romanian supervision, while preserving auditability of data processing and compatibility with governance-oriented deployment settings. The proposed pipeline is designed as a structured-data access component for AI-assisted administrative systems rather than as a standalone conversational interface. Its purpose is to support onboarding of new administrative databases while remaining compatible with controlled workflows, authorization checks, and institutional infrastructure. Pilot evaluation results indicate measured improvements in executable SQL generation compared to a base Romanian model, with the strongest raw gain observed after in-domain fine-tuning. At the same time, the lower raw execution-result match and the reliance on bounded repair indicate that semantic correctness remains the main challenge. The contribution is therefore a locally deployable and execution-grounded method for extending AI-assisted public services toward structured-data access under constraints of privacy, data sovereignty, and predictable infrastructure requirements.
ER  - 

