AB  - Blockchain systems are undergoing a fundamental transition from decentralized ledgers for digital assets to general-purpose trust infrastructures for verifiable computation, decentralized physical resources, and automated infrastructure management. However, conventional Blockchain-as-a-Service models often rely on externally managed computing infrastructure, introducing additional trust, flexibility, and data-sovereignty dependencies. Meanwhile, the open, modular, and extensible RISC-V instruction set architecture has emerged as a promising execution and hardware co-design substrate for blockchain workloads. This survey introduces embedded Blockchain Infrastructure Management (eBIM) as a conceptual hardware–software co-design framework that organizes existing RISC-V-enabled technologies into a hardware-anchored blockchain infrastructure stack. We systematically review RISC-V-based smart-contract execution, trusted execution environments, zero-knowledge virtual machines, and cryptographic acceleration, and analyze their relationship to blockchain requirements including deterministic execution, verifiability, extensibility, and secure key management. Based on this evidence, we distinguish currently demonstrated capabilities from the proposed eBIM integration vision and identify major open challenges, including system-level benchmarking, blockchain-oriented cryptographic extensions, TEE security, post-quantum migration, and deployable hardware–software co-design. The survey provides an architectural reference for researchers and designers investigating open and verifiable blockchain infrastructure.
