AB  - Understanding the interplay between an individual's genetic makeup and environmental and lifestyle factors as determinants of individual health and disease condition critically relies on having access to and the integrated analysis of genomics data that are linked to health and exposure data from different populations and continents. Requirements for hosting such data in biobanks go beyond ethical and legal compliance and need solutions that create mutual trust. For this reason, a trusted research environment for genetic, health, and exposure data has to be established, comprising several pillars. It must (i) build on a legal basis (e.g., in Europe, the General Data Protection Regulation) as well as on ethical principles; (ii) be centered around patient engagement; (iii) use innovative cyber security and privacy preserving technologies; (iv) apply appropriate governance models, which together; (v) lead to data sovereignty; and (vi) enable fair benefit sharing. In addition, there is a need for new data access solutions for collaborative research that enable research on patient-related data within a secure, trusted environment and do not require sending data outside of this environment to researchers in different countries. The inclusion of patient-related data, particularly from low- and middle-income countries, in international collaborative research is a prerequisite that solutions developed will also serve the needs of people in these countries. This creates an additional challenge since most low- and middle-income countries do not have the required analytical capacities to develop products and generate economic benefit from data use. Therefore, fair benefit sharing models should counterbalance inequalities between the efforts of data providers and benefits for data users.
