UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772503026000290
KW  - Data protection
KW  - Algorithmic profiling
KW  - Inferential privacy
KW  - Cultural values
KW  - Regulatory frameworks
KW  - Chama
AB  - Kenya's digital economy has expanded rapidly since the Data Protection Act of 2019, with platforms such as M-Pesa, Tala, and Huduma Namba becoming integral to financial transactions and identity management. While these technologies have broadened access to services, they raise concerns about algorithmic profiling and privacy risks in a setting where economic life is largely communal. This study developed a culturally informed framework to evaluate how inferential profiling affects collective financial practices in Kenya. Adopting a qualitative, interpretive approach, the research analysed 114 publicly available sources, including policy documents, court rulings, and regulatory reports, that formed the primary analytical corpus. Through thematic analysis, the study examined how profiling mechanisms interact with communal rights and traditional economic arrangements. The findings reveal misalignments between digital profiling systems and local cultural norms, alongside regulatory gaps in enforcement by the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner. The proposed framework comprises three analytical tools: the Cultural Inference Matrix, which identifies how algorithms misinterpret group-based practices; the Behavioural Fingerprinting Index, which traces behavioural adaptations prompted by profiling pressures; and the Communal Privacy Impact Score, which evaluates protections for collective consent and transparency. Drawing on Indigenous data sovereignty scholarship and digital colonialism critiques, the framework addresses a broader gap in data governance literature regarding the Global South. By bridging this gap, the framework provides a foundation for more inclusive and culturally aware approaches to data governance, ensuring that regulatory measures better reflect communal financial practices and collective rights.
